**Wiring**

| SSD1306 pin | ESP32 pin | Notes |
|-------------|-----------|-------|
| VCC         | 3.3 V     | Do **not** feed 5 V into a typical OLED module; most modules are 3.3 V tolerant. |
| GND         | GND       | |
| SDA         | GPIO 21   | Default I²C data pin on the ESP32’s primary I²C bus. |
| SCL         | GPIO 22   | Default I²C clock pin on the ESP32’s primary I²C bus. |
| RES (reset) | –         | Many modules have it hard‑wired to VCC; if not, connect to any spare GPIO and set low for a brief reset in code. |
| DC/CS       | –         | Ignored on I²C modules. |
| RST (optional) | –     | Same as RES. |

If your module does **not** have built‑in pull‑up resistors on SDA/SCL, add 4.7 kΩ (or 10 kΩ) pull‑ups to VCC.

---

### Software

1. **Install the libraries**

   * Arduino IDE → Sketch → Include Library → Manage Libraries…  
     Search for “Adafruit SSD1306” and install it along with “Adafruit GFX”.

2. **Include headers**

   ```cpp
   #include <Wire.h>
   #include <Adafruit_GFX.h>
   #include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>
   ```

3. **Define the display object**

   The SSD1306 is 128 × 64 pixels on most modules.

   ```cpp
   #define SCREEN_WIDTH 128 // OLED width, in pixels
   #define SCREEN_HEIGHT 64 // OLED height, in pixels

   // I²C address: 0x3C is the default; if you have soldered a pin for 0x3D use that instead.
   #define SSD1306_I2C_ADDRESS 0x3C

   Adafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT,
                            &Wire, -1); // -1 means no reset pin
   ```

4. **Setup**

   ```cpp
   void setup() {
     Serial.begin(115200);

     // Initialise I²C bus (optional if using default pins)
     Wire.begin();          // SDA=21, SCL=22

     // Begin the display driver
     if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, SSD1306_I2C_ADDRESS)) {
       Serial.println(F("SSD1306 allocation failed"));
       for (;;); // Halt
     }

     display.clearDisplay();
     display.setTextSize(1);
     display.setTextColor(WHITE);
     display.setCursor(0, 0);
     display.print("Hello ESP32");
     display.display();   // Push the buffer to the screen
   }
   ```

5. **Loop**

   ```cpp
   void loop() {
     // Your code here
   }
   ```

---

### Things to double‑check

| Item | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| I²C address | Most modules default to 0x3C; if you solder the “SCL” or “A0” pin, change to 0x3D. |
| Pull‑ups | Without them the bus may float and communication will fail. |
| Power rail | 5 V can damage a 3.3 V OLED module unless it has built‑in level shifting. |
| Reset pin | If you use an external reset, set its GPIO low for ~10 ms during initialization. |

That’s all you need to get an SSD1306 OLED working with an ESP32 over I²C.

